[checkpoint] Judge the library (complete)
Read the suite and decide whether command and served tool follow.
Serves: the delivery ruling put the decision here rather than at the start.
Absent: the next two stages get built because they were mentioned once.
Known by: a decision recorded with what it was made from.
-> mend-refusals
-> mend-rot
-> mend-slice
-> mend-surface
-> tests
> The library checkpoint is met by 123 unit tests and 10 dependency-facing tests passing, with cargo fmt --all -- --check, cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings, cargo doc --workspace --no-deps, and cargo deny check passing on 2026-08-21. cargo deny reports duplicate-version warnings but no policy failures. The separate command remains outside this graph; MCP is the next delivery and its contract and execution record are in human-authored-specs/mcp.md and mcp.vine.
---
[mend-slice] Make an oversized chunk unconstructible (complete)
Hold the chunk limit where it cannot be forgotten.
Serves: vocabulary.md says a chunk too large to embed cannot be constructed, and nothing enforces it.
Absent: add files a chunk whose embedding describes a prefix of its own text, with no refusal and no error.
Known by: a text built to force two consecutive skipped cuts yields either a chunk within limit or a refusal, never a truncated embedding; and a text whose cuts never advance does not collapse into one chunk.
-> slice
-> types
> The adversary is two consecutive skipped cuts: three windows merged is 254 x 3 + 2 specials = 764 tokens against a 512 limit. Ordinary prose will not produce it, so the test has to construct it.
> Truncation at the embed seam is silent today. Either make it loud or make it unreachable; a silent prefix is the one outcome the ladder exists to rule out.
> Measured before anything was designed, because the cutting rule rested on two facts nobody had checked. The tokenizer's offsets are byte offsets into the original text, not character offsets - `e` acute at the front of a string comes back as (0, 2). And one character can carry several tokens: a Hangul syllable is decomposed to three jamo and comes back as three tokens all beginning at the same offset, while kana and Latin advance one character at a time.
> The adversary this node names is not reachable through this tokenizer, and that is the finding rather than the fix. A skipped cut needs a whole window of tokens - 255 of them - to share one start offset, which needs one character the tokenizer spends 255 tokens on; the longest run measured is three. So the merged chunk was a hole in the argument, not a defect anyone could reach, and the rule was still resting on an argument rather than on anything that enforces it.
> The grid is gone. A window is now measured from the cut before it rather than from a grid laid over the whole text, and the loop runs while more than a window of tokens is left - so a chunk holding more than a window is the exit condition rather than a check standing beside it. Under the grid, a candidate that could not be cut at handed its tokens to the next chunk and the grid marched on regardless, which is how two in a row handed on three windows.
> A candidate that cannot be cut at is an error rather than passed over. Stepping back to an earlier token was written and then deleted: a candidate only fails when every token from the chunk's first shares one start, and every earlier candidate in that window shares it too - so the step back could never find anything, and it was machinery for a case it could not serve. The one lawful answer is to say so, since cutting inside a character is not a span at all and a second cutting rule is what the slice node ruled out.
> The check that a chunk fits the model lives at the embed seam and nowhere else. Slice cuts to the budget, but a chunk re-tokenized on its own can drift, and a query never passes through slice at all - so the count that decides belongs where the model is, taken on the exact string the model will read. Slice's guarantee and embed's check are two claims, not one written twice: slice's is about the cut it produced, embed's is about the text it is handed.
> Embed asks its own tokenizer rather than holding a second one. fastembed's tokenizer is configured to truncate at the model's limit, and a truncated encoding carries what was dropped - so the question `would this be truncated` is put to the tokenizer that would do the truncating. No threshold is restated in embed.rs and the truncation-off configuration is not written twice. slice.rs still holds the only tokenizer that counts.
> The query path was the reachable defect, and closing it is what this node actually bought. An agent's query goes straight to embed, so a long query was answered with an embedding of its first 512 tokens and nothing said so; no amount of care in slice could have caught it. The chunk path was already safe by argument, which is why the fix had to be at the seam rather than at the cut.
> It is io::Error with InvalidInput, not a sixth refusal. A chunk over the limit is a defect in slice, and a query over it is a caller handing the model more than it accepts - both mistakes rather than decisions about material an agent named, which is the reading the store node gave the same shape.
> Both acceptance criteria are driven against the rule with offsets built by hand, because no text this tokenizer answers reaches them. The run in the test is 510 tokens on one character, which is exactly the two consecutive skipped cuts this node names. That the old rule would have merged three windows there is derived rather than measured, since the rule that did it is gone.
> Measured by bisection rather than assumed: `kettle` is one token under this pin, 510 of them are embedded and 511 are refused, so the boundary the tokenizer enforces is the 512 the model accepts once the two it adds are counted. That pair is now a test, so the instrument carries the known answer it was checked against.
> Superseded, and recorded here rather than rewritten there: the slice node's decision that a candidate which does not advance is passed over rather than adjusted, and its reasoning that the slack absorbs the merge. The slack does absorb one merge and not two, which is what this node was opened for.
> Measured: 130 tests pass, 10 of them slice's and 7 embed's, in 4.7s for the library and 5.4s for the suite from the warm model cache. cargo clippy passes the workspace's pedantic set over all targets, cargo fmt is clean, and cargo deny is clean - advisories, bans, licences and sources. Cargo.lock did not move and no dependency arrived; two files were edited and no other part was reached past.
---
[mend-surface] Narrow the surface to the contract (complete)
Narrow the public surface to the verbs the vocabulary names.
Serves: architecture.md says no verb enumerates the corpus and only add and delete write.
Absent: the integration suite passes because the API is wider than the contract it claims to measure.
Known by: the crate exposes open and the five verbs and nothing else reachable from outside; the suite either compiles without reaching past them or fails loudly.
-> store
-> tests
> The suite reads whole document text through Store::document. If narrowing breaks the suite, that is the finding rather than an obstacle - a test that needs a hatch is measuring the hatch.
> Vector::as_slice, Embedding::vector and Model::new are on the same list: architecture.md says nothing outside embed sees a vector, and a public Model::new lets a fresh store be stamped with a name no Embed produces.
> The line drawn at this stage, before mend-rot removed the unused cache helper, was: public is open, the five verbs, the two seams a caller must build to call them - Store::cache, Embed::load, Embed::model, Slice::new - the constructors a caller must reach for to name material (Origin::parse, Query::new, Count::new, Text) and read-only accessors on the values in flight. Everything that constructs what the corpus is made of, or reads it back out, is crate-visible.
> The fork not taken, recorded so it is reviewed rather than rediscovered: open could have taken only a path and loaded the model itself, which is literally what vocabulary.md writes and would have closed Embed and Slice too. This node's own second decision rules against it - a public Model::new only lets a store be stamped wrongly if a caller is holding a model, so the shape it is written against is the one where the caller holds the seams. The narrower open is a change to what open is, not to what is reachable from it, and it belongs to a node that says so.
> After narrowing, Store::replace, Store::document and Store::scan are pub(crate), which is the ruling the tests node left as data. Fetch is crate-private as well and is the only reach to the network, through add.
> Model stays exported and has nothing public on it: no constructor, no name, no dimension, no limit. Store::open names the type and Embed::model is the only thing that yields one, so a store can only be stamped by a model this build loaded. Document, Embedding, Vector, Limit and Window are not exported at all - no signature a caller can call names them - and the production table in lib.rs now writes those rows without a link and says why, so the one-for-one check still passes by eye.
> The constructors of the values scry mints - Chunk, Handle, Digest, Passage, Hit, Provenance, Incompatible - are pub(crate), which narrows the types node's ruling that constructors are public. Nothing was taken away: Span::new was already crate-private, so a caller could not build a Span and therefore could not reach any of them. What is removed is public surface that was already uncallable. The types node's reason still stands where it matters - a Handle that came back from a hit is still just a claim, refused when it disagrees with the text at its span.
> Handle::mint and Handle::verify, Score::cosine, Text::span and Text::at, Slice::spans, and Embed's passages, query and tokenizer_file are pub(crate). Minting is what scry chooses to hand out and verifying is a verb's judgement; the rest take or return something a caller cannot name.
> Embed::passage is deleted rather than demoted. Once the seam closed it had no caller in the crate - add embeds a document's chunks in one batch - so it was dead code in a shipped build. Its four tests take one passage out of the batch through a helper, which is the path add actually runs.
> The suite's walk back to the bytes now reads the file the origin names and cuts it at the span, instead of reading the document out of the store. That is the claim provenance makes and the one architecture.md writes down - the path back to bytes runs through origin, into the world, not through our file - so the test got stronger by losing the hatch. It is also why Origin::as_path and Span::start and end stay public.
> Where the suite asserted nothing was filed by asking the store for a document, it now asks search and expects nothing back. No verb enumerates, so the only reading of the corpus a caller has is the one that answers a query.
> Incompatible is provoked by a file at a store's path that this build did not write, rather than by a corpus met by another model. A caller can no longer name a model this build does not hold, which is the narrowing working rather than a case going untested - the store's own tests still stamp a second model and meet it, and they can, because they are inside the crate. What the suite keeps is that the end is reached, that it names the path, and that it is that file and not this build which ended it.
> Limit::tokens and Window::tokens now take self by value. clippy's trivially_copy_pass_by_ref exempts public API, so the lint only appeared once those types stopped being exported - the narrowing measured something the wide surface had been hiding.
> Measured: 134 tests pass, 124 the library's and 10 the suite's, in 6.4s and 8.2s from the warm model cache. cargo clippy passes the workspace's pedantic set over all targets, cargo fmt is clean, cargo doc has no warnings - the production table's links were the thing that would have broken quietly - and cargo deny is clean over advisories, bans, licences and sources. Cargo.lock did not move and no dependency arrived.
