[mend-refusals] Fix what answers wrongly at the edges (complete)
Fix the places that answer wrongly at the edges.
Serves: four claims are held by code that gives the wrong answer on inputs nobody tried.
Absent: a corrupt stored vector mis-ranks silently, a malformed url becomes a key, an overflowing ttl reads fresh, and a corrupt store is reported as weather rather than as a store this build cannot use.
Known by: each has a test that provokes it deliberately.
-> fetch
-> origin
-> store
> Four adversaries, each named: a stored vector that parses as finite floats but is not the one written; the url http://[::1 with an unclosed bracket; a ttl whose addition to fetched_at overflows; a redb file that is corrupt rather than foreign.
> The digest covers the text half of a record and nothing covers the vector half. That asymmetry is the bug, not the renormalisation.
> The record covers its own bytes, and LAYOUT rose to 2 because the shape changed. The whole record rather than the vector half alone: a cover over one half needs a boundary, the boundary is read off the record, and a length that moved is one of the things being guarded against - so both halves are read back on the same terms and there is nothing to place wrongly. A store written under 1 is another layout and says so at open.
> The vector adversary is provoked twice in one test, because refusing it only means something beside what accepting it costs. A float is overwritten with another finite float pointing elsewhere and the record is refused; then the same altered bytes are given a checksum of their own and decode, and what comes back is an embedding nobody wrote, with no refusal and no error. The second half is the price of the first, measured rather than argued.
> A record found wrong during a verb is io::Error InvalidData and not Incompatible. The vocabulary gives Incompatible to open alone, so corruption caught at open is the end and corruption caught at the first read of one document is weather at the point of use. Recorded rather than hidden: the two answers are the same fact reaching scry through two doors, and only one of them has a word.
> An authority that opens a bracket must close it, or the input named no document. Read as a bare host, http://[::1 split at its last colon into the host [: and the port 1, and came back out as http://[::1/ - a key whose spelling names something the caller did not write and which parse would read differently again. http://[] went the same way. Only closure is ruled here: a host carrying a space or a non-ASCII label is still a key, and refusing those is a policy this node was not given and would change which documents scry accepts.
> expired measures the material's age against the ttl rather than now against a deadline. An age is a duration between two moments that both exist, so nothing leaves the range the platform can hold and there is no answer to invent.
> Data, and it is the honest half of that adversary: the wrong answer is not reachable with any clock. An addition that overflows puts the deadline past every moment a SystemTime can hold, so now can never be past it, and false was already the answer a deadline would have given. What was wrong is that the answer was defaulted rather than computed - is_some_and reads if I cannot tell, say fresh, which is the pattern the ladder rules out whether or not a caller can see it. The test states the provocation as an assertion that the sum is None, so it is standing on the adversary rather than near it.
> Corruption is the end, superseding the store node's ruling and recorded here rather than rewritten there. That node declined to fold it in because corrupt is not another model or another layout, which reads the word by its cause; this reads it by what it does. Nothing proceeds, no verb runs, and no second attempt mends it - while weather says try again, which corruption never will. Incompatible's own sentence widened to `the store at X is not one this build can use', and which of the three it is changes nothing an agent can do.
> Left for the checkpoint rather than settled here: vocabulary.md names two causes for Incompatible and there are now three. The human-authored specs are not mine to edit, and the widened sentence lives in incompatible.rs where a reader meets it.
> One classification, not four. Every redb failure on the open path leaves as redb's own error and is read once by fault(), which answers end or weather - so the judgement is in one place instead of at each call site, and stamped() converts nothing. A corrupt read there answers false, which is already the answer that sends open to the end.
> Measured about redb, and recorded because it bounds what this node bought: over single-byte flips across the first 96 bytes of a store, one offset answers Corrupted, one reads as another layout, 16 panic inside redb, and the rest open unchanged because the bytes were never allocated. A file truncated in half panics too. A panic inside a dependency is not something the crate-wide denial of panic reaches, so `a corrupt store opens for nobody' holds for the corruptions redb reports and not for the ones it dies on. The test names the offset it found and how it was found, since redb publishes no layout to read it off.
> Reviewed the three nodes this depends on before starting, as the root asks. store's record was where two of the four lived and its own decision named the asymmetry; origin's split_host_port was the one place a url's authority is read, and its rule that None is for input naming no document is what an unclosed bracket now takes; fetch was reviewed and not touched - it absorbs every origin it cannot read as NotFound, so a url refused at parse never reaches it and no refusal moved. provenance was reached past, and it is not among the dependencies: expired is the one implementation of the projection and the types node put it there.
> Measured: 134 tests pass, 124 the library's in 4.9s and 10 the suite's in 8.6s from the warm model cache. cargo clippy passes the workspace's pedantic set over all targets, cargo fmt is clean, and cargo deny is clean - advisories, bans, licences and sources. Cargo.lock did not move and no dependency arrived: the record's checksum is hmac-sha256, which the handle node already declared.
