[mend-rot] Delete what nothing calls (complete)
Delete what nothing calls, and share what everything rewrites.
Serves: deletion is the cheapest operation available and seven items have no callers.
Absent: readers spend attention on code that does nothing, and duplicated test scaffolding drifts.
Known by: the crate builds with them gone, and what survives is reached by a verb or a test.
-> tests
> At review start, Store::cache was the notable one: it existed so the store and the model cache could not name different disks, and five call sites hardcoded a temp path instead. Either the callers used it or it went - the hazard it prevented was realised inside the crate that wrote the remedy.
> Test scaffolding is rewritten four to seven times and has already drifted: pair() in store.rs and the same construction inlined differently in delete.rs.
> Re-measured rather than taken from the node, by counting call sites across the crate and the suite: four items have no caller now where seven did when this was written. What is gone is Span::len, AddRefusal::origin, HandleRefusal::origin, and Store::cache with the CACHE constant and the one test that reached it. The other three already had callers or had already left, so the count is what moved and not the reading.
> Store::cache goes. The property it defends - the store and the weights naming one disk - has no stated cost of loss, and nothing in the stamp or the record depends on where the weights sit; the only way for a caller to honour it is a cache per store, which the crate's own tests priced at a 133 MB fetch per test and declined. An invariant nobody will pay for should not keep its defense. A caller names the cache when it loads Embed, which is what every call site in the crate and the suite already did.
> The two refusal accessors and Span::len were second ways to read a fact already handed over: the refusal variants are public and carry the origin, and a span's start and end are public. Nothing called any of them, and no test lost a claim by their going.
> The scaffolding lives in one cfg(test) module, scaffold, which ships in no build and is exported nowhere, as fixture does. What moved there is what more than one module was writing for itself - the cache, the scratch directory, the model, the store, the origin, the hand-built document, its embeddings, filed, held, minted and count. What stayed is what one module alone says: the wrapper around its own verb, and the text it is measured over.
> A scratch directory takes the caller's own module_path!(), so two modules that name a test the same way get a directory each. Before, each module hand-wrote scry-<part>-<test>, which is the same collision answered by remembering; delete and store both had a test called absent.
> One fetched_at for every hand-built document, and it is not the epoch. provenance's tests read it back off a document built on the epoch, so a fetched_at dropped anywhere between the record and the read would have come back as the value it started from and said nothing. store's own builder already used a non-zero moment; that is the one that survived.
> The embeddings a hand-built document is filed with are the two axes, alternating, rather than one vector repeated. Measured while making them differ: a vector normalised from [1,1] does not read back out of the record bit for bit, because decode renormalises, and the scan test that compares what was read against what was written failed on it. The axes are what survive that renormalisation exactly, so the comparison stays an equality rather than becoming a tolerance.
> Measured: 133 tests pass, 123 the library's in 6.6s and 10 the suite's in 8.0s from the warm model cache. One test fewer than before, which is the one that reached Store::cache. cargo clippy passes the workspace's pedantic set over all targets, cargo fmt is clean, cargo doc has no warnings - lib.rs named Store::cache in the seams a caller builds - and cargo deny is clean over advisories, bans, licences and sources. Cargo.lock did not move and no dependency arrived. 582 lines left and 60 arrived across the files that were there, against about 200 in the one new one.
